 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(V \cdot R)$, where $V$ is the number of views in `camlist` and $R$ is the cost of rendering a single view. The method requires two renders per view (one for loss, one for metric accumulation) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing `full_metric_counts` and `full_metric_score`, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Additional memory for intermediate render buffers per view [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Explicitly targets regions with persistent multi-view inconsistency, which are often indicative of missing geometry or artifacts. The binary thresholding reduces sensitivity to absolute loss magnitude variations across views [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires multiple renders per optimization step, increasing computational overhead. Sensitive to the choice of `loss_thresh` [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When standard gradient-based densification fails to resolve multi-view inconsistencies or artifacts in texture-dense regions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are severely constrained, or when the scene is static and well-reconstructed with standard methods [S1, unofficial].
